#: ../../../tutorials/basics/polygons.rst:22
msgid "Plotting polygons"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/polygons.rst:24
msgid "Plotting polygons is handled by the :meth:`pygmt.Figure.plot` method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/polygons.rst:26
msgid ""
"This tutorial focuses on input data given as NumPy arrays. Besides NumPy "
"arrays, array-like objects are supported. Here, a polygon is a closed shape "
"defined by a series of data points with x- and y-coordinates, connected by "
"line segments, with the start and end points being identical. For plotting "
"a :class:`geopandas.GeoDataFrame` object with polygon geometries, e.g., to "
"create a choropleth map, see the gallery example :doc:`Choropleth map </"
"gallery/maps/choropleth_map>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/polygons.rst:35
msgid ""
"import numpy as np\n"
"import pygmt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/polygons.rst:44
msgid "Plot polygons"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/polygons.rst:46
msgid "Set up sample data points as NumPy arrays for the x- and y-values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/polygons.rst:50
msgid ""
"x = np.array([-2, 1, 3, 0, -4, -2])\n"
"y = np.array([-3, -1, 1, 3, 2, -3])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/polygons.rst:59
msgid ""
"Create a Cartesian plot via the :meth:`pygmt.Figure.basemap` method. Pass "
"arrays to the ``x`` and ``y`` parameters of the :meth:`pygmt.Figure.plot` "
"method. Without further adjustments, lines are drawn between the data "
"points. By default, the lines are 0.25-point thick, black, and solid. In "
"this example, the data points are chosen to make the lines form a polygon."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/polygons.rst:67
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[-5, 5, -5, 5], projection=\"X5c\", frame=True)\n"
"fig.plot(x=x, y=y)\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/polygons.rst:78
msgid ""
"The ``pen`` parameter can be used to adjust the lines or outline of the "
"polygon. The argument passed to ``pen`` is one string with the comma-"
"separated optional values *width*,\\ *color*,\\ *style*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/polygons.rst:84
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[-5, 5, -5, 5], projection=\"X5c\", frame=True)\n"
"# Use a 2-point thick, darkred, dashed outline\n"
"fig.plot(x=x, y=y, pen=\"2p,darkred,dashed\")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/polygons.rst:96
msgid ""
"Use the ``fill`` parameter to fill the polygon with a color or :class:"
"`pattern <pygmt.params.Pattern>`. Note, that there are no lines drawn "
"between the data points by default if ``fill`` is used. Use the ``pen`` "
"parameter to add an outline around the polygon."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/polygons.rst:103
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[-5, 5, -5, 5], projection=\"X5c\", frame=True)\n"
"# Fill the polygon with color \"orange\"\n"
"fig.plot(x=x, y=y, fill=\"orange\")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/polygons.rst:117
msgid "Close polygons"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/polygons.rst:119
msgid ""
"Set up sample data points as NumPy arrays for the x- and y-values. Now, the "
"data points do not form a polygon."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/polygons.rst:124
msgid ""
"x = np.array([-2, 1, 3, 0, -4])\n"
"y = np.array([-3, -1, 1, 3, 2])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/polygons.rst:133
msgid "The ``close`` parameter can be used to force the polygon to be closed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/polygons.rst:137
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[-5, 5, -5, 5], projection=\"X5c\", frame=True)\n"
"fig.plot(x=x, y=y, pen=True)\n"
"\n"
"fig.shift_origin(xshift=\"w+1c\")\n"
"\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[-5, 5, -5, 5], projection=\"X5c\", frame=True)\n"
"fig.plot(x=x, y=y, pen=True, close=True)\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/polygons.rst:153
msgid "When using the ``fill`` parameter, the polygon is automatically closed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/polygons.rst:157
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[-5, 5, -5, 5], projection=\"X5c\", frame=True)\n"
"fig.plot(x=x, y=y, pen=True)\n"
"\n"
"fig.shift_origin(xshift=\"w+1c\")\n"
"\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[-5, 5, -5, 5], projection=\"X5c\", frame=True)\n"
"fig.plot(x=x, y=y, pen=True, fill=\"orange\")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

